Dean, we disagree, and I want the reason on the record rather than in a mee=
ting.<br />
<br />
ASTM F1869 measures emission from roughly the top half inch of slab. On lig=
htweight concrete fill over metal deck it reads low and it reads low reliab=
ly, because the surface skin dries long before the body of the fill does. T=
hat is precisely the condition on floors 12 through 19. ASTM F2170 in-situ =
probes read the slab at depth, which is why our specification is written to=
 F2170 and why the 85% threshold is stated in RH and not in pounds.<br />
<br />
Fourteen probes at depth read 92 to 96%. Nine surface tests read low. Those=
 results are not in conflict, they are measuring different parts of the sam=
e wet slab, and only one of them tells you what the adhesive will see over =
twenty years.<br />
<br />
One more thing. Your 5 pound limit is the carpet tile adhesive. The resilie=
nt tile and the heat welded sheet are published at 3 pounds, and five of yo=
ur nine kits came back at 3.0 or above. Your own data does not clear the pr=
oducts it is being used to clear.<br />
<br />
Our recommendation is unchanged. Section 09 05 61 stays.